      "problem": "${(-\\frac{1}{5})}^{2}=$",
      "worked_solution": "First, notice that $-\\frac{1}{5}=\\frac{(-1)}{5}.$\n\nTo raise the fraction to the power of $2$, we must raise both the numerator and the denominator to the power of $2:$\n\n$$\n{(-\\frac{1}{5})}^{2}={(\\frac{(-1)}{5})}^{2}=\\frac{(-1)^{2}}{{5}^{2}}\n$$\n\nNow, using $(-1)^{2}=1$ and ${5}^{2}=25,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{(-1)^{2}}{{5}^{2}}=\\frac{1}{25}.\n$$",
